# infra/optimism-mainnet/.env — Ophis Optimism mainnet secrets.
# COPY this file to .env, fill in the secrets, NEVER COMMIT the populated form.
# .gitignore already excludes infra/optimism-mainnet/.env.
#
# After copying, chmod 600 .env — render-configs.sh enforces this on every
# deploy but the initial copy is on the operator. This keeps the
# OKX credentials owner-only at the filesystem layer.
#
# NOTE (2026-05-18, Tier 1 PK isolation): the driver-submitter PK is no
# longer stored in the macOS Keychain or this .env file. It lives at
# /Users/ophis-driver/.config/submitter.key (0600, owned by ophis-driver
# user). render-configs.sh reads it via sudo at deploy time.
#
# NOTE (2026-05-19, Phase 4 audit M H3 honesty fix): OKX, CoinGecko, and
# Postgres credentials live HERE in this .env at chmod 600. The earlier
# comment claimed OKX creds live in Keychain — that was aspirational,
# never implemented. .env at chmod 600 is the actually-working path.
# Threat model: any process running as scep can read .env. Tier 1.5
# (RAM-disk render) or Tier 2 (per-secret manager) closes this. Until
# then, keep .env on FileVault-encrypted disk + Mac mini's physical
# security as the only barriers.

# ── Database ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Pick a strong random password (32+ chars). Different from testnet (which
# uses '123' for convenience). Generate via:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
POSTGRES_USER=ophis
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_DB=ophis
DB_WRITE_URL=postgresql://ophis:${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}@db:5432/ophis
DB_READ_URL=postgresql://ophis:${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}@db:5432/ophis

# ── Chain RPC ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Phase 4 audit M-2 (2026-05-19): chain reads now flow through the
# eRPC proxy at http://rpc-proxy:4000/main/evm/10 (configured in
# configs/erpc.yaml.tmpl, 3 upstreams = 2 keyed (zan + alchemy) + publicnode,
# 2-of-3 consensus on critical methods). The OP_MAINNET_RPC env var
# below is no longer wired into
# any service by default — left as a hint for operators who want to
# bypass eRPC for a quick smoke test:
#   OP_RPC_INTERNAL=https://optimism-rpc.publicnode.com docker compose up -d
OP_MAINNET_RPC=https://optimism-rpc.publicnode.com

# Optional override: if set, ALL chain-reading services point at this URL
# instead of the eRPC proxy. Used for failure-domain tests (e.g. point at
# a single upstream to validate fallback behavior) and emergency bypass if
# eRPC itself misbehaves. Leave empty in normal operation.
OP_RPC_INTERNAL=

# ── Keyed eRPC consensus upstream secrets ──────────────────────────────────
# The OP eRPC quorum is 2 KEYED providers (zan + alchemy) + free publicnode
# (see configs/erpc.yaml.tmpl). Only the secret KEY goes here — the host+path are
# LITERAL in the template so the fail-closed CI guard (assert-erpc-failclosed.py)
# can pin the failure domain via urlsplit. render-configs.sh substitutes these
# into rendered/erpc.yaml (RAM-disk, never committed).
#
# LEGACY (not in the current quorum; harmless to leave unset): validationcloud's
# client has been disabled since it hit its limit in July 2026.
VALIDATIONCLOUD_OP_KEY=your-validationcloud-optimism-key
# REQUIRED since 2026-08-29 — render-configs.sh refuses to render without it.
# blockdaemon IS Cloudflare-fronted, which disqualified it NEXT TO publicnode
# (two CF lanes break the <=1-CF invariant). It is fine INSTEAD OF publicnode:
# it now holds the single CF slot, so the CF count is unchanged at one.
# It is the only FULL-ARCHIVE lane in the quorum -- measured 2026-08-29,
# eth_getTransactionReceipt OK and eth_call/eth_getLogs OK at 2,000,000 blocks
# behind head, where publicnode returned -32602 even 200 blocks back. That is
# what gives receipts and deep getLogs a THIRD voter instead of two, so the
# quorum survives losing the zan lane to its monthly credit cap.
# Sustained vet 2026-08-29: 644/644 = 100% over 200s at ~3.2 req/s uncacheable,
# zero non-200 in any 30s bucket, p50 171ms.
# NOTE the key rides in a QUERY PARAM (?apiKey=), so an empty substitution does
# NOT trip the trailing-'/' or '//' checks -- validate_rendered_erpc has a
# dedicated empty-query-param rule for it.
BLOCKDAEMON_OP_KEY=your-blockdaemon-optimism-apikey
# dRPC keyed lane (2026-08-30) — REQUIRED, holds the single Cloudflare slot.
# REPLACED blockdaemon, whose free tier hit "Quota limit exceeded" after ONE DAY.
# Both are Cloudflare-fronted, so the swap is domain-neutral.
# Free tier is 210M CU / 30 days at ~20 CU per request. This stack sends ~5.5M
# requests/30d to one lane => ~110M CU = ~52% of allowance: the first CF-slot lane
# with real headroom. ⚠️ The free tier caps THROUGHPUT (120k CU/min per IP), not
# volume — do NOT probe it above ~3 req/s or you drain the day's budget and the
# measurement lies. Verified 633/633 = 100% over 360s at 3 req/s, production mix.
# Key rides in a QUERY PARAM (?dkey=); an empty value is caught by both
# validate_rendered_erpc and the render-time fail-fast.
#   https://lb.drpc.org/ogrpc?network=optimism&dkey=${DRPC_API_KEY}   chainId 10
DRPC_API_KEY=your-drpc-api-key
# Tenderly KEYED Node RPC gateway (2026-08-29) — REPLACED official-op
# (mainnet.optimism.io), which was returning 7.5% HTTP 429 at a sustained 4 req/s
# and is archive-gated. Both are Google-fronted (AS396982, `via: 1.1 google`), so
# the swap is domain-NEUTRAL and the three-independent-domain property holds.
# ⚠️ NEVER add tenderly ALONGSIDE official-op: two Google lanes would let one
# provider satisfy agreementThreshold:2 — the Cloudflare attack, different logo.
# Keyed limit is 400 req/s (keyless 20). An EMPTY value silently degrades to the
# keyless gateway, so render-configs.sh refuses to render without it.
#   https://optimism.gateway.tenderly.co/${TENDERLY_OP_KEY}   chainId 10
TENDERLY_OP_KEY=your-tenderly-node-rpc-access-key
# Alchemy keyed OP-mainnet lane (2026-08-09, replaced tenderly). ACCOUNT-WIDE
# credential (macOS keychain: alchemy-api-key) — the same account's L1 lane
# feeds mainnet polling elsewhere, and the shared monthly quota is what
# exhausted it in July (resets on the 1st). If it 429s, replace the lane
# IMMEDIATELY: the degraded zan+publicnode state still satisfies 2-of-3 for
# Block A/B and tie-breaks disputes across only two capable lanes
# (preferBlockHeadLeader — single-compromise protection lost; see the
# template's 2026-07-30 P1 note). Only receipts fail closed. Never loosen
# consensus parameters to compensate.
#   https://opt-mainnet.g.alchemy.com/v2/${ALCHEMY_API_KEY}   chainId 10
ALCHEMY_API_KEY=your-alchemy-api-key
# ZAN (Ant Digital Technologies) keyed OP-mainnet node — the one AUTHENTICATED
# lane in the 2026-07-30 quorum. Registered-account key; the unregistered tier
# hard-rejects eth_call / eth_getLogs / eth_estimateGas (-32012), which left those
# methods with a two-lane quorum and no single-compromise protection.
# Renamed from ZAN_OP_KEY 2026-07-30: the credential is ACCOUNT-WIDE, not
# OP-specific. One key serves several chains and only the URL path differs, so an
# OP-flavoured name was misleading for anyone wiring up another stack:
#   https://api.zan.top/node/v1/opt/mainnet/${ZAN_API_KEY}   chainId 10   (used here)
#   https://api.zan.top/node/v1/eth/mainnet/${ZAN_API_KEY}   chainId 1
#   https://api.zan.top/node/v1/base/mainnet/${ZAN_API_KEY}  chainId 8453
#   https://api.zan.top/node/v1/arb/one/${ZAN_API_KEY}       chainId 42161  (note: arb/one)
# Unichain is NOT offered by zan. All four verified 2026-07-30.
ZAN_API_KEY=your-zan-node-key

# ── Driver-submitter private key ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Corresponds to on-chain address 0x92B9bE5e96795E8630fDC61efb0e705E75b1A1B1,
# which was allowlisted in deploy-mainnet-all.sh Step 3 (addSolver). This EOA
# signs every settlement transaction. Treat like a hot-wallet key — keep
# balance bounded (~0.02 OP ETH, top up as needed) to cap blast radius.
#
# DEPRECATED post-Tier-1 PK isolation (2026-05-18). The PK now lives at
# /Users/ophis-driver/.config/submitter.key and is read by render-configs.sh
# directly. Leave this var empty in production .env.
OPHIS_DRIVER_SUBMITTER_KEY=

# ── OKX aggregator solver credentials ─────────────────────────────────────
# All 4 fields are required by apps/backend/crates/solvers OKX module.
# Create at https://web3.okx.com/build → API keys → New key with scopes:
# Trade + Read on the OnchainOS DEX product. Copy each field below.
#
# IMPORTANT: do NOT add a payment method on the OKX dashboard. The API
# caps at the $100 Basic + $50 Premium monthly free quota and stops
# responding (rather than billing) once exceeded. Caps financial risk
# at $0/mo.
#
# Rotation procedure if leaked: OKX dashboard → revoke old key → create
# new with same scopes → update lines below → run render-configs.sh →
# `docker compose restart okx-solver`. Verify with a sample quote via
# orderbook /api/v1/quote.
OKX_PROJECT_ID=
OKX_API_KEY=
OKX_SECRET_KEY=
OKX_PASSPHRASE=


# Enso aggregator (free Bearer key, sent as Authorization). Rendered into
# enso.toml. Self-serve at https://developers.enso.build. REQUIRED: an empty
# value makes enso-solver restart-loop (EmptyApiKey).
ENSO_API_KEY=

# ── Distributed tracing ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Phase 4 audit M-6 (2026-05-19). The CoW backend's observe crate ships
# opentelemetry-otlp + tonic gRPC export already; docker-compose now runs
# a Jaeger all-in-one (image: jaegertracing/all-in-one) bound to:
#   - http://jaeger:4317  OTLP/gRPC ingest (services use this URL)
#   - http://127.0.0.1:16686  Jaeger UI (operator-facing)
#
# Set the env var below to enable tracing:
#   TRACING_COLLECTOR_ENDPOINT=http://jaeger:4317
#
# Leave empty to disable (tracing_config() returns None and the binaries
# skip the OTLP exporter entirely — zero overhead).
#
# In-memory trace storage: history wipes on jaeger restart. Acceptable
# for current "debug a specific incident" use; promote to persistent
# Badger storage when retention >24h is needed.
TRACING_COLLECTOR_ENDPOINT=

# ── Observability (Prometheus + Alertmanager → Telegram) ─────────────────
# Alertmanager sends `critical` / `warning` alerts to Clement's Telegram
# DM via the Clawdius bot. Token shape: {int}:{base64-ish}.
#
# Phase 1.5 (2026-05-20): PREFERRED token source is macOS Keychain,
# NOT this .env file. Keeps the token off cleartext disk, out of Time
# Machine backups, out of casual `cat .env` exposure.
#
# Setup (one-time):
#   security add-generic-password -a "$USER" -s ophis-telegram-bot \
#     -w '<bot-token>' -U
#
# render-configs.sh checks the Keychain when TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN is unset
# in .env. Token lookup procedure for the value:
#   ~/.openclaw/openclaw.json → channels.telegram.botToken
#
# Leave this var EMPTY when you've put the token in Keychain. Set it
# here only if you have a one-off reason (e.g. testing with a different
# bot) — render-configs.sh respects env-var over Keychain.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=

# ── Tenderly REST API (Phase 3 functional completeness) ──────────────────
# 2026-05-20: Clement's Tenderly account (san-npm) REST API key.
# Used for pre-broadcast settlement simulation — driver verifies that
# `settle(...)` will succeed BEFORE paying gas. Catches reverts before
# the mempool, saves the 5-gwei-cap budget on bad solutions.
#
# Endpoint: https://api.tenderly.co/api/v1/account/san-npm/project/project/
# Header:   X-Access-Key: ${TENDERLY_API_KEY}
#
# NOT used for the eRPC Gateway. Web3 Gateway needs a separate access
# key (tenderly.co → Web3 Gateway → New Endpoint → Optimism Mainnet).
# When you create that, set TENDERLY_GATEWAY_URL below.
TENDERLY_API_KEY=

# When you create a Tenderly Web3 Gateway for OP, paste the full URL here
# (e.g. https://optimism.gateway.tenderly.co/<gateway-id>). erpc.yaml.tmpl
# will use it as a 3rd upstream and we can re-add eth_getBalance to strict
# consensus.
TENDERLY_GATEWAY_URL=

# ── CoinGecko (native-price oracle for autopilot + orderbook) ─────────────
# Required by docker-compose.yml — autopilot and orderbook fail-fast at
# startup if unset. Get a free key at
# https://www.coingecko.com/en/api/pricing — the free Demo tier allows
# 30 calls/min, 10K calls/month, enough for OP-only operation.
COINGECKO_API_KEY=

# ── Inter-service auth (F7, 2026-05-21 whole-repo audit) ──────────────────
# Shared Bearer token between autopilot ↔ driver. When set, the driver
# enforces `Authorization: Bearer <token>` on all per-solver routes
# (/solve /reveal /settle /quote /<info>). Autopilot injects the header
# on every outgoing request. When unset (current default), both services
# log a warning at startup and the routes are unauthenticated — the
# pre-F7 posture, mitigated only by docker-network isolation.
#
# THREAT MODEL: a compromised co-tenant container on the docker network
# can drive settlements directly via unauthenticated routes. Today's
# stack has no such co-tenant, but Spec 8 brings a V3 solver service
# onto the same network. Set this BEFORE Spec 8 lands.
#
# Setup (one-time, identical value on both driver + autopilot):
#   TOKEN=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
#   security add-generic-password -a "$USER" -s ophis-inter-service-auth-token -w "$TOKEN" -U
#   # Then pass into the stack via this .env (do NOT paste the token here —
#   # source it from the Keychain at runtime via a wrapper, e.g. on stack-up):
#   #   export OPHIS_INTER_SERVICE_AUTH_TOKEN=$(security find-generic-password \
#   #     -s ophis-inter-service-auth-token -w)
#   #   ./compose-up.sh
#
# Format check: token must be non-empty for enforcement. Use ≥ 32 random
# bytes (64 hex chars).
OPHIS_INTER_SERVICE_AUTH_TOKEN=

# Eth-flow auto-refunder gas key (dedicated low-value EOA; keychain ophis-refunder-pk).
# Address 0x6fdc54717176C57b675C85fa08E9B92f44448Dbb. See docs/operations/ethflow-refunder.md
OPHIS_REFUNDER_PK=

# ─── eRPC image pin (NOT a secret; recorded here so the version is in git) ───
#
# docker-compose.yml requires ERPC_IMAGE to be pinned as
# ghcr.io/erpc/erpc:<version>@sha256:<digest>. The live value lives only in
# .env, which is gitignored, so before 2026-09-04 nothing in the repository
# recorded which eRPC we actually ran and nothing stopped it drifting back.
#
# 2026-09-04: upgraded 0.0.64 -> 0.2.0. eRPC is open source and the image is
# public on ghcr.io; this costs nothing and needs no account.
#
# WHY THE UPGRADE MATTERS -- do NOT roll back below 0.1.2. 0.0.64 carries an
# implicit cached-head availability gate that rejects an upstream BEFORE
# forwarding whenever its polled head trails the network-known highest. The
# state poller is request-driven, so the busiest lane defines the tip, can
# never fail its own check, and the quieter lanes are excluded on roughly
# every new block. Rejection tracked TRAFFIC SHARE, not lateness: measured
# with all three lanes on the IDENTICAL block, tenderly 0%, drpc 32%,
# zan 90% ErrUpstreamBlockUnavailable. That silently reduced a 2-of-3 quorum
# to one lane on the settlement-gating path.
#
# Upstream deleted the gate in d655d63e3db1, "fix(evm): block availability
# gating enforces only configured bounds" (#934), 2026-06-17 -- after 0.1.1.
# After the upgrade ErrUpstreamBlockUnavailable is ZERO on every lane and
# zan-op went from 90% errors to none.
#
# Rollback pin, only if 0.2.0 itself misbehaves. This is 0.1.2, the FIRST
# release containing d655d63 -- NOT the pre-upgrade 0.0.64. Rolling back to
# 0.0.64 during an incident would reintroduce the availability gate and thin
# the settlement quorum to one lane at exactly the worst moment:
#   ghcr.io/erpc/erpc:0.1.2@sha256:c728ea76ffdd50f6adf6ffadb224a74ecb720b41c4748fad3688ff44db3f7955
ERPC_IMAGE=ghcr.io/erpc/erpc:0.2.0@sha256:c6a22d3290cc54bef0dd0b915d779c5218d049563151c267e5c69c9adc644d5c
